/**
 * @page evaluation_shape_constraints Evaluation and shape constraints
 *
 * This page is the contract for Operon's two-phase evaluator path and for
 * shape-constraint enforcement. It is intentionally specific: the evaluator
 * interface is a hot path, and a change that adds an extra forward pass or
 * bypasses the @c EvaluatedBuffer proof changes both cost and correctness.
 *
 * @section evaluation_entrypoints Entrypoints and phase contract
 *
 * @c EvaluatorBase::operator()(rng, individual, scratch) is the only composed
 * three-argument entrypoint. It is @c final and always calls @c Evaluate before
 * @c Score. The two-argument facade owns a training-range-sized scratch buffer
 * and forwards to that entrypoint.
 *
 * This UML activity view distinguishes actions, the evaluation-result decision,
 * and terminal outcomes.
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph evaluator_phases {
 *   rankdir=TB;
 *   graph [bgcolor="transparent", pad=0.25, nodesep=0.45, ranksep=0.7];
 *   node [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10];
 *   edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, color="#4a5568", fontcolor="#4a5568", arrowsize=0.7];
 *   start [shape=circle, label="", width=0.18];
 *   evaluate [shape=box, style="rounded", label="EvaluatorBase::Evaluate(ind, scratch)"];
 *   outcome [shape=diamond, label="Interpreter error?"];
 *   proof [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Optional EvaluatedBuffer"];
 *   score [shape=box, style="rounded", label="EvaluatorBase::Score(context, proof)"];
 *   error [shape=box, style="rounded", label="ErrMax"];
 *   fitness [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Fitness vector"];
 *   finish [shape=doublecircle, label="", width=0.18];
 *   start -> evaluate -> outcome;
 *   outcome -> error [label="yes"];
 *   outcome -> proof [label="no"];
 *   proof -> score -> fitness -> finish;
 *   error -> finish;
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * @c Evaluate has three outcomes:
 *
 * - @c tl::unexpected<InterpreterError> means evaluation failed. The composed
 *   entrypoint increments @c CallCount and returns @c ErrMax.
 * - An empty @c std::optional means this evaluator has no value buffer to pass
 *   to @c Score, or a wrapper rejected the individual without an interpreter
 *   failure.
 * - A present @c EvaluatedBuffer proves that this exact @c Individual was
 *   evaluated into the supplied scratch prefix.
 *
 * @c EvaluatedBuffer is move-only and can be minted only by
 * @c EvaluatorBase::MarkEvaluated. A value-based @c Score implementation must
 * read it with @c Values(individual, scratch); this verifies both identities
 * before exposing the values. @c ScoreContext carries the RNG, individual, and
 * scratch span for the duration of that call. It must not be stored.
 *
 * Every @c Score override increments its own @c CallCount exactly once. Counter
 * meaning is therefore phase-specific: @c ResidualEvaluations counts forward
 * work performed by a concrete evaluator, while @c CallCount counts completed
 * score-phase attempts plus the base fallback for an @c Evaluate error.
 *
 * @section shape_roles Shape-constraint roles
 *
 * @c ShapeConstrainedEvaluator wraps an @c EvaluatorBase. It hard-rejects a
 * tree that cannot be certified over the configured domain and otherwise
 * delegates scoring to the wrapped evaluator. @c ShapeViolationEvaluator is a
 * separate one-objective evaluator: it measures violation and does not wrap a
 * score evaluator.
 *
 * A @c ShapeConstraintMeasurement records whether a bound was certified, its
 * finite interval when available, and its violation. A
 * @c ShapeConstraintMeasurementSummary is feasible only when every constraint
 * is certified with zero violation.
 *
 * @section shape_hard_reject Hard-reject flow
 *
 * This UML activity view makes the feasibility branch explicit: rejection still
 * enters the wrapper score phase, but never calls the wrapped scorer.
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph shape_hard_reject {
 *   rankdir=TB;
 *   graph [bgcolor="transparent", pad=0.25, nodesep=0.45, ranksep=0.7];
 *   node [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10];
 *   edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, color="#4a5568", fontcolor="#4a5568", arrowsize=0.7];
 *   start [shape=circle, label="", width=0.18];
 *   gate_eval [shape=box, style="rounded", label="ShapeConstrainedEvaluator::Evaluate"];
 *   inner_eval [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Wrapped Evaluate\n(one forward pass)"];
 *   result [shape=diamond, label="Interpreter error?"];
 *   certify [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Certify feasibility\n(fit scaling from values on cache miss)"];
 *   feasible [shape=diamond, label="Feasible?"];
 *   gate_score [shape=box, style="rounded", label="ShapeConstrainedEvaluator::Score"];
 *   inner_score [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Wrapped Score"];
 *   reject [shape=box, style="rounded", label="WorstValue\nviolations_++"];
 *   error [shape=box, style="rounded", label="ErrMax"];
 *   finish [shape=doublecircle, label="", width=0.18];
 *   start -> gate_eval -> inner_eval -> result;
 *   result -> error [label="yes"];
 *   result -> certify [label="no"];
 *   certify -> feasible;
 *   feasible -> gate_score [label="yes"];
 *   feasible -> gate_score [label="no: empty proof"];
 *   gate_score -> inner_score [label="feasible"];
 *   gate_score -> reject [label="infeasible"];
 *   inner_score -> finish;
 *   reject -> finish;
 *   error -> finish;
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * The wrapper always invokes its wrapped evaluator's @c Evaluate first. If that
 * evaluator supplied an @c EvaluatedBuffer, @c FeasibleFromValues computes the
 * linear-scaling fit from its existing values and then measures constraints; it
 * does not run another interpreter pass. If the wrapped evaluator is not
 * value-based, the wrapper falls back to tree-based @c Feasible.
 *
 * Infeasibility returns an empty proof from the wrapper's @c Evaluate. The base
 * entrypoint still calls the wrapper's @c Score. That score phase increments the
 * gate's @c CallCount, observes the warm feasibility cache, increments
 * @c violations_, returns a @c WorstValue vector, and does not call the wrapped
 * evaluator's @c Score. The wrapped evaluator's forward-pass counters can still
 * grow by one; hard rejection skips scoring, not the shared forward pass.
 *
 * @section shape_bounds Bound construction and enforcement
 *
 * Shape constraints are evaluated over a domain box, not sampled points. For an
 * identity constraint, the original tree is bounded. For a derivative
 * constraint, @c BuildVariableGradientDag constructs the derivative DAG before
 * it is bounded. The per-constraint raw bound is transformed by the fitted
 * linear scaling when scaling is enabled, then checked against the requested
 * sign or interval. An unavailable or non-finite bound is uncertified and
 * contributes @c UnknownViolation.
 *
 * @c ShapeBoundMode selects the enclosure backend:
 *
 * - @c Interval is the default. It uses interval arithmetic only and shares one
 *   @c IntervalEvaluator across a constraint set.
 * - @c Combined intersects affine and interval results.
 * - @c Affine isolates affine arithmetic for published ablation compatibility.
 * - @c Bisected adds interval-domain subdivision and requires @c Interval.
 *
 * @c MeasureConstraints builds one first-derivative DAG lazily when needed and
 * shares it across all derivative constraints. It shares one interval or affine
 * evaluator across that constraint set. This is the boundary where bound-mode
 * cost belongs; it must remain separate from the ordinary interpreter forward
 * pass used for fitness.
 *
 * @section shape_cache Cache lifecycle and limits
 *
 * @c ShapeConstrainedEvaluator caches a
 * @c ShapeConstraintMeasurementSummary by a coefficient-aware tree hash and
 * @c ShapeBoundMode. @c Feasible and @c FeasibleFromValues use the same cache,
 * so the score phase normally observes the entry created during its preceding
 * evaluate phase. @c Prepare delegates to the wrapped evaluator and clears the
 * feasibility cache. @c SetBoundOptions also clears it because options are not
 * part of the memoization key.
 *
 * The cache is an execution aid, not a source of semantic state. Never extend
 * its lifetime across generations without a documented capacity or eviction
 * policy. Coefficient-aware keys prevent stale answers after coefficient
 * changes; they do not bound memory for a stream of distinct trees.
 *
 * @section shape_design_boundaries Design boundaries
 *
 * - Linear scaling is part of the certified model when it is enabled for the
 *   problem. A fast path that certifies only the raw tree is a different
 *   semantic mode, not a transparent optimization.
 * - Hard rejection is a gate around scoring. It preserves the forward-pass
 *   result needed for certification and only suppresses the wrapped score.
 * - @c Feasible is a public standalone query. A direct @c Score call must still
 *   re-check it, because that caller may have skipped @c Evaluate.
 * - Constraint certification is pessimistic: an unprovable bound is not
 *   accepted as feasible.
 *
 * @section shape_api API reference starting points
 *
 * - @c EvaluatorBase, @c EvaluatedBuffer, and @c ScoreContext in
 *   @c include/operon/operators/evaluator.hpp.
 * - @c ShapeConstrainedEvaluator, @c ShapeViolationEvaluator,
 *   @c ShapeConstraintPolicy, @c ShapeBoundMode, and @c ShapeBoundOptions in
 *   @c include/operon/operators/shape_constrained_evaluator.hpp.
 * - Bound execution and enforcement in
 *   @c source/operators/shape_constrained_evaluator.cpp.
 */
